From de108430fe012fc7318449dcef175a4ee13bcd77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexis Williams <alexis@typedr.at>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:36:25 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 12/17] Build the tools and the Unix parts WebAssembly can take

"Add support for WebAssembly" leaves out every Unix-specific source and
guards out every Unix path. Some of those work under Emscripten as they
do on other Unixes, others left callers behind, and GLib's tools didn't
build. Bring back what WebAssembly can take, and work around what it
lacks.

Resolve the references to the sources that stay out. A static link
garbage-collects their callers, but a main module keeps every function
for side modules to import, so the link failed on undefined symbols:
- Build giounix.c, which is plain POSIX I/O on file descriptors and
  provides an API giochannel.h declares for every Unix (gtestutils.c and
  gtestdbus.c call g_io_channel_unix_new()). GIOChannel watches take
  source closures again.
- g_unix_connection_send_fd() and the D-Bus command line's stdin used
  gunixfdmessage.c and gunixinputstream.c. Guard them like the
  neighbouring receive_fd and GApplicationCommandLine stdin, which
  already report no support.
- gappinfo.c called the application database's *_impl functions from
  gdesktopappinfo.c. Give them empty implementations: there are no
  applications to find or launch.

Build glib-unix.c and GIO's Unix streams. Emscripten's file descriptors
(files, pipes, /dev/null) take them as they are; the rest of the Unix
sources need mounts, desktop files or D-Bus.
- glib-unix.c is built without the Unix signal sources, which
  glib-unix.h no longer declares there: gmain.c handles no signals on
  WebAssembly, where no other process can send one.
  g_unix_fd_query_path() fails with G_FILE_ERROR_NOSYS, since
  Emscripten's file systems have no /proc nor another way to name an
  fd's file. GUnixFDSource takes closures again.
- gfiledescriptorbased.c, giounix-private.c and the Unix input and
  output streams are built, and their headers and gio-unix-2.0.pc
  installed.
- _g_fd_is_pollable(): Emscripten declares epoll but fails every call,
  and its poll() only follows pipes and sockets, so those are what is
  pollable.

Restore three paths that work as on other Unixes:
- g_source_add_unix_fd(), g_source_modify_unix_fd(),
  g_source_remove_unix_fd() and g_source_query_unix_fd(), which gmain.h
  declares on every Unix. They only add fds to the source's poll set.
- Local files' standard::is-hidden and standard::is-backup: dotfiles,
  names listed in a directory's .hidden file and names ending in "~".
  Lost+found directories, which need the Unix mount table, are not
  looked for.
- A content type for files in a GResource, guessed from the name and
  data, as for local files.

Build the tools. Three link against Unix sources that stay out: on
WebAssembly gtester opens its report pipe with pipe() (spawning the test
binary then reports that it's unsupported), gio info leaves out the Unix
mount line, gio launch reports that it isn't supported on this platform,
as on Windows and macOS, gapplication list finds no D-Bus activatable
applications, and list-actions reports that it can't find the
application's desktop file. The tools run under Node with its file
system, so configure's /proc/self/cmdline run check would pass there,
although the library also runs in browsers, which have no /proc; take
its result from the have_proc_self_cmdline cross property on
Emscripten, as for a cross build that can't run host programs.

Work around what WebAssembly lacks:
- g_test_trap_subprocess() runs the test binary again as a child,
  which WebAssembly can't spawn, so a test program aborted at its first
  subprocess test. Mark the calling test skipped and jump back out of
  its test function to test_case_run(), so the program goes on with its
  other tests; returning would leave the caller asserting on a
  subprocess that never ran. A call from any other thread, or outside a
  test case, only marks the test skipped.
- g_local_file_query_exists() calls faccessat() with AT_EACCESS and
  AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW, which Emscripten doesn't honour: its JavaScript
  file systems assert that the flags are 0 or AT_EACCESS (so every
  g_file_query_exists() aborted with runtime assertions) and WasmFS
  ignores the flag, so a dangling symlink can be reported missing. Leave
  query_exists unset, as on the platforms whose faccessat() has the same
  gaps, so it falls back to querying the file's type without following
  symlinks.
- With no argv, g_option_context_parse() names the program from the
  platform's record of its first argument, which WebAssembly had none
  of. Emscripten's libc sets program_invocation_name from the script
  Node runs, or from the page's Module.thisProgram.
- Emscripten's dlfcn.h defines RTLD_NEXT, so HAVE_RTLD_NEXT was set, but
  dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, ...) finds nothing there: in a static link dlsym()
  fails for every handle, and with dynamic linking RTLD_NEXT is
  unsupported (and aborts). Only tests use it.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Wasm-specific]
